      "claim": "L5 human jejunal perfusion shows very low effective permeability for related ionizable peptidomimetics: enalaprilat 0.013 ± 0.009 ×10^-4 cm/s (C63) and inogatran 0.03 ×10^-4 cm/s (C64), indicating poor intestinal absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C63", "C64"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "A related amidine and carboxylate compound shows very poor permeability and a high efflux ratio of 46 in Caco-2 (C68), providing gut-wall efflux evidence for presystemic loss in this chemical class.",
      "card_ids": ["C68"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C70 notes dabigatran is not a P-gp substrate, a caveat that efflux is not universal among benzamidines; this does not contradict the low prediction because the query has more extreme polarity and no active transport evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C70"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Atenolol low-permeability standards (C61, C62, C66, C67, C71, C72) confirm low Papp is associated with incomplete human absorption, and saquinavir (C69) shows moderate Papp can still yield low F due to presystemic loss.",
      "card_ids": ["C61", "C62", "C66", "C67", "C69", "C71", "C72"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
